      "answer": "Yes. According to the official SQLite documentation, foreign key enforcement is off by default and must be turned on for each connection.\n\nSupporting original sentence:\n\n> \"Foreign key constraints are disabled by default (for backwards compatibility), so must be enabled separately for each database connection.\"\n\nThe page explains this in Section 2, \"Enabling Foreign Key Support,\" noting that the application enables enforcement at runtime with `PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON;`. It also adds that future releases might change the default, so developers should explicitly enable or disable foreign keys rather than assume.\n\nSource: [SQLite Foreign Key Support](https://sqlite.org/foreignkeys.html)",
