      "answer": "According to MDN’s table on the cited page:\n\n| Method | Safe | Idempotent | Cacheable |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `GET` | Yes | Yes | Yes |\n| `POST` | No | No | Conditional* |\n| `DELETE` | No | Yes | No |\n\nThe table’s asterisk note states:\n\n> “`POST` and `PATCH` are cacheable when responses explicitly include freshness information and a matching `Content-Location` header.”\n\nThus, POST is neither safe nor idempotent and only conditionally cacheable; DELETE is not safe, is idempotent, and is not cacheable; GET is safe, idempotent, and cacheable.\n\nSource: [MDN Web Docs — HTTP request methods](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Methods)",
